   "answer": "**Result: the age-adjusted death rate rose from 2019 to 2020. Men have a higher age-adjusted rate than women.**\n\n**What I did**\n1. I read the rate file. It has 5 groups and 11 age groups, with age-specific rates and the 2000 United States standard population (no counts).\n2. I ran direct standardization on all 5 groups. The 2000 standard population gave the weights.\n3. I ran the ratio of the 2020 rate to the 2019 rate, and the ratio of the male rate to the female rate, with the same standard.\n4. I checked the standardized rates with a separate calculation from the file weights. The values matched.\n5. I checked the weights. They have 11 age groups and total 1,000,000.\n\n**Results** (direct method, 2000 United States standard, 11 age groups, rates per 100,000)\n\n| Group | Age-adjusted rate per 100,000 |\n|---|---|\n| All causes, 2020 | 835.42 |\n| All causes, 2019 | 715.24 |\n| Heart disease, 2020 | 168.19 |\n| Male, all causes, 2020 | 998.34 |\n| Female, all causes, 2020 | 695.06 |\n\n- Ratio of the 2020 rate to the 2019 rate (all causes): 1.168. The numerator is 2020. The reference is 2019.\n- Ratio of the male rate to the female rate (2020, all causes): 1.436. The numerator is male. The reference is female.\n\n**What I measured and what I infer**\n- The rates and ratios are measured from the file. They are standardized rates, not crude rates.\n- The file has rates only. The tool gives no confidence interval and no test. I cannot say if the 2020 rise or the male-female gap is statistically significant.\n- The ratios describe the values in the file. They do not show a cause.\n\n**Uncertain or to check**\n- The file does not name the denominator of the rates (population or person-years). I report the rates per 100,000 as the file states.\n- I did not check the individual age-specific values for errors. The data check flagged some extreme values. I kept all values.\n- The tool reported its recorded standard as \"us2000_11\". I passed the file's weights. My calculation with these weights gives the same values. Please confirm that this standard is the one you want.\n\n**What waits for you**\n- An interval for each rate and each ratio needs counts of deaths and the population of each age group. It also needs a choice of interval method (for example exact or gamma). If you can supply the counts, I can compute intervals.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Rate multiplier: 100000 · Significance level of the intervals: 0.05 · Direct or indirect standardization: direct · Standard population for direct standardization: us2000_11 · Interval of the standardized ratio: exact · Source of the reference rates: not applicable.",
